"03_struct_accessors.mst — fields the struct generator could not size.

 `tools/dolphin2mst/genstructs.py` reads field offsets from winkb
 (Win32Metadata) and emits a typed accessor per field. Where it cannot
 determine a field's WIDTH it emits a comment instead:

     fMask: Windows.Win32.UI.WindowsAndMessaging.MENUINFO_MASK at 4 — no
     accessor (width unknown); use byteAt: directly

 That is the right refusal — guessing a width silently writes the wrong number
 of bytes and corrupts the neighbouring field — but it leaves a hole, and
 `UI.Menu>>applyFlags` falls in it: `struct style: flags` on a MENUINFO whose
 `dwStyle` has no accessor.

 THE MISSING WIDTHS ARE ENUM TYPES. `MENUINFO_MASK` and `MENUINFO_STYLE` are
 Win32 enums, and a Win32 enum in a struct is a DWORD — 32 bits — unless it
 declares otherwise. winkb records the enum's name but the generator does not
 follow it to its underlying type.

 GENERATOR FIX, recorded in docs/LOOSE_ENDS.md: resolve an enum-typed field to
 its underlying integer type and emit the accessor. Until then the accessors
 live here, HAND-CHECKED against the offsets the generator did emit — the
 fields on either side pin them exactly:

     cbSize   uint32 at 1   (offset 0)
     fMask    ?      at 5   (offset 4)   <- supplied here
     dwStyle  ?      at 9   (offset 8)   <- supplied here
     cyMax    uint32 at 13  (offset 12)

 A wrong width here would show up immediately as a corrupted `cyMax`, which is
 why the neighbours are quoted: they are the check."
ExternalMemory subclass: MENUINFO [
    fMask [ ^self uint32At: 5 ]
    fMask: v [ ^self uint32At: 5 put: v ]
    dwStyle [ ^self uint32At: 9 ]
    dwStyle: v [ ^self uint32At: 9 put: v ]

    "Dolphin's own spellings. `Menu>>applyFlags` writes `struct style: flags`,
     and `Menu>>queryStyle` reads it back."
    style [ ^self dwStyle ]
    style: v [
        "MIM_STYLE = 16r10. Setting the style without ALSO setting the mask
         bit that says 'the style field is meaningful' makes SetMenuInfo
         ignore it — the call succeeds and nothing changes, which is the
         quietest possible failure. Dolphin own MENUINFO style: sets
         both; this does the same rather than leaving it to the caller."
        self fMask: (self fMask bitOr: 16r10).
        ^self dwStyle: v
    ]
]

"MENUITEMINFOW — the same enum-width gap, three fields, and the Smalltalk
 protocol Dolphin puts on top of the layout.

 Offsets pinned by the neighbours the generator DID emit:

     cbSize   uint32 at 1   (offset 0)
     fMask    ?      at 5   (offset 4)    <- supplied
     fType    ?      at 9   (offset 8)    <- supplied
     fState   ?      at 13  (offset 12)   <- supplied
     wID      uint32 at 17  (offset 16)

 `commandMenuItem:metrics:` and `basicText:` are Dolphin's own, from
 `OS.MENUITEMINFOW.cls`, transcribed rather than translated: that `.cls` also
 carries a `defineFields` template, and emitting it as a class would REPLACE
 the generated layout with one that has no offsets at all — the same shape as
 the `.pax` incident that overwrote this very struct in DD10.

 IMAGES ARE NOT SUPPORTED and that is why the image branch is absent rather
 than stubbed: `setImage:metrics:` needs the Graphics wave (DD11). A menu item
 with an image will get its text and id and no bitmap, which is a visible
 difference, not a silent one."
ExternalMemory subclass: MENUITEMINFOW [
    fMask [ ^self uint32At: 5 ]
    fMask: v [ ^self uint32At: 5 put: v ]
    fType [ ^self uint32At: 9 ]
    fType: v [ ^self uint32At: 9 put: v ]
    fState [ ^self uint32At: 13 ]
    fState: v [ ^self uint32At: 13 put: v ]
    hSubMenu: v [ ^self intptrAt: 25 put: v ]
    dwTypeData: v [ ^self intptrAt: 57 put: v ]

    "Dolphin's `basicText:`. The buffer must OUTLIVE the SetMenuItemInfo call
     that reads through the pointer, which it does because the caller holds
     this struct until the send returns — the same lifetime rule the create
     path relies on for lpClassName."
    basicText: aString [
        | buf |
        buf := Utf16Buffer fromString: aString displayString.
        self dwTypeData: buf address.
        ^buf
    ]

    "MIIM_STRING = 16r40, MIIM_ID = 16r2, MIIM_FTYPE = 16r100, MIIM_SUBMENU
     = 16r4."
    "Dolphin's `type:` — set fType and mark ONLY that field meaningful.
     `DividerMenuItem>>populateItemInfo:metrics:` uses it: a separator has no
     text and no id, so the mask must not claim it has.
     MIIM_FTYPE = 16r100, assigned rather than OR-ed, exactly as Dolphin does."
    type: anInteger [
        self fType: anInteger.
        ^self fMask: 16r100
    ]

    commandMenuItem: aCommandMenuItem metrics: aSystemMetrics [
        | mask fType |
        mask := 16r40 bitOr: 16r2.
        fType := aCommandMenuItem styleFlags.
        fType = 0 ifFalse: [
            self fType: fType.
            mask := mask bitOr: 16r100 ].
        self basicText: aCommandMenuItem text.
        self wID: aCommandMenuItem id.
        self fMask: mask.
        ^self
    ]
]
